FILE: vUX-resizer.js
FEATURE: resizer-example
DATE: 2026-08-27
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'resizer-fixes'

================================================================================
CHANGE 1 — Replace a generated handle stylesheet instead of appending another
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
        if(style != ""){
            //Scoped to the axis, otherwise the x and y rules overwrite one another.
            var selector = "."+handlerClass+"."+axis;
            var css = selector+":hover{"+style+"}";
            css += selector+".on{"+style+"}";
            attachStyleSheet(axis+"Resizehandler", css);
        }

NEW_SECTION
        if(style != ""){
            //Scoped to the axis, otherwise the x and y rules overwrite one another.
            var selector = "."+handlerClass+"."+axis;
            var css = selector+":hover{"+style+"}";
            css += selector+".on{"+style+"}";
            //Replaced rather than appended: attachStyleSheet() appends unconditionally, so a
            //Resizer torn down and rebuilt would leave a dead <style> behind on every cycle.
            var previous = $$.ss("style[data-id='"+axis+"Resizehandler']");
            if(previous != null && previous.parentNode != null) previous.parentNode.removeChild(previous);
            attachStyleSheet(axis+"Resizehandler", css);
        }

REASON
Found by building the example, not by reading the module — the playground rebuilds its
Resizer on every control change, which made the accumulation obvious within a dozen
clicks.

attachStyleSheet() appends a <style data-id="..."> unconditionally; it does not check
whether one with that data-id is already present. Under the pre-fix module this was
invisible, because initialize() was effectively called once and never again. destroy()
makes rebuild-in-place a normal thing to do, and every rebuild that configures a
handle style left the previous <style> element in <head> for ever. A page with a
control panel over a Resizer accumulates one dead node per axis per change.

Removing the same-data-id element first keeps <head> at one stylesheet per axis. It
does not alter the resolution order between two Resizers configured with different
styles — that was already last-writer-wins, since both generate rules against the same
global `.resizeHandle.x` / `.resizeHandle.y` selectors. The example's README states
that limitation and gives the workaround (a distinct handle class per instance via
config.myResizeHandler) rather than pretending it is solved.

The fix is deliberately local to this module. attachStyleSheet() is a shared helper in
src/helpers.js used by other components, and changing its append semantics for
everyone is a much larger change than this one warrants.
